=begin pod :kind("Type") :subkind("class") :category("basic")

=TITLE package RakuAST::Literal

=SUBTITLE Represent a literal value

    class RakuAST::Literal { }

The C<RakuAST::Literal> class represents a literal value (such as `0`
or `"foo").  It also serves as a base class for specific literal values:

=item L<C<RakuAST::IntLiteral>|/type/RakuAST/IntLiteral> - literal C<Int> value
=item L<C<RakuAST::NumLiteral>|/type/RakuAST/NumLiteral> - literal C<Num> value
=item L<C<RakuAST::StrLiteral>|/type/RakuAST/StrLiteral> - literal C<Str> value
=item L<C<RakuAST::RatLiteral>|/type/RakuAST/RatLiteral> - literal C<Rat> value
=item L<C<RakuAST::ComplexLiteral>|/type/RakuAST/ComplexLiteral> - literal C<Complex> value
=item L<C<RakuAST::VersionLiteral>|/type/RakuAST/VersionLiteral> - literal C<Version> value

=head2 Object introspection

C<RakuAST::Literal> objects are typically created when parsing Raku
Programming Language code.  So most developers will only need to know
how to introspect the objects created.

These introspection methods are available (in alphabetical order):

=head3 value

=begin code :preamble<my $literal>
say $literal.value;
=end code

The C<value> method returns the value it is representing.

=head2 Object creation

There is one way to create a C<RakuAST::Literal> object.

=head3 from-value

=begin code
my $literal = RakuAST::Literal.from-value(42);
say $literal.^name;  # RakuAST::IntLiteral
=end code

Creates a C<RakuAST::Literal> object from the given value.  Note that for
some core types, specialized subclasses will be returned:

=item C<Int> value -> C<RakuAST::IntLiteral>
=item C<Num> value -> C<RakuAST::NumLiteral>
=item C<Str> value -> C<RakuAST::StrLiteral>
=item C<Rat> value -> C<RakuAST::RatLiteral>
=item C<Complex> value -> C<RakuAST::ComplexLiteral>
=item C<Version> value -> C<RakuAST::VersionLiteral>

In all other cases a C<RakuAST::Literal> object will be returned.

=end pod
